{
  "slug": "liaison",
  "number": 14,
  "part": 4,
  "title": "Liaison",
  "tagline": "Why words run together, which sound appears and when it's required or forbidden.",
  "goal": "Understand why words join up in speech, know which sound appears in each liaison and when it's required, optional or forbidden.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "que-es",
      "title": "What liaison is",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Many French words end in a **silent** consonant: *les* («lay»), *vous* («voo»), *petit* («ptee»). But if the next word starts with a **vowel or silent h**, that consonant **is pronounced** and attaches to the next word:"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "les + amis → les‿amis",
          "caption": "Pronounced «lay-za-mee»."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "English does something similar with «an apple» versus «a pear». It's why spoken French often sounds like one long word."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sonidos",
      "title": "The sounds of liaison",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The written letter doesn't always sound the same in liaison:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Written",
            "Sounds",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**s, x, z**",
              "**[z]**",
              "*les‿amis* (lay-za-mee) · *deux‿enfants* (duh-zahn-fahn) · *chez‿elle*"
            ],
            [
              "**t, d**",
              "**[t]**",
              "*petit‿ami* (ptee-ta-mee) · *grand‿homme* (grahn-tom)"
            ],
            [
              "**n**",
              "**[n]**",
              "*un‿ami* · *mon‿enfant*"
            ],
            [
              "**f** (in *neuf*)",
              "**[v]**",
              "*neuf‿ans* · *neuf‿heures*"
            ],
            [
              "**r**",
              "**[r]**",
              "*premier‿étage*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "An s or x in liaison always buzzes like [z], never hisses like [s]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obligatorias",
      "title": "Required liaisons",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Case",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Determiner + noun",
              "*les‿amis, un‿ami, mon‿école, ces‿enfants, deux‿heures, des‿hôtels*"
            ],
            [
              "Pronoun + verb",
              "*nous‿avons, vous‿êtes, ils‿ont, on‿a, elles‿aiment*"
            ],
            [
              "Verb + pronoun (inversion)",
              "*Vont‿ils ? Est‿il ?*"
            ],
            [
              "Adjective before the noun",
              "*petit‿enfant, grand‿homme, bon‿ami, beaux‿yeux*"
            ],
            [
              "After short prepositions",
              "*chez‿elle, dans‿un parc, en‿Afrique*"
            ],
            [
              "Set phrases",
              "*Comment‿allez-vous ? · tout‿à coup · de temps‿en temps · les États‿Unis*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The [z] that separates *ils‿ont* from *ils sont* is a liaison [[4]]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "prohibidas",
      "title": "Forbidden liaisons",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Case",
            "Examples (│ = no liaison)"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "After *et*",
              "*toi et │ elle · un chat et │ un chien*"
            ],
            [
              "Before an aspirated h",
              "*les │ héros · les │ hiboux · en │ haut*"
            ],
            [
              "After a singular noun",
              "*un étudiant │ intelligent · un enfant │ adorable*"
            ],
            [
              "Before *oui*, *huit* and *onze*",
              "*mais │ oui · les │ huit jours · les │ onze joueurs*"
            ],
            [
              "After a proper name",
              "*Paris │ est beau · Thomas │ arrive*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "title": "Heroes and zeros",
          "text": "With a liaison, *les héros* would sound like *les zéros* (the zeros). It's the classic example of the aspirated h [[3]]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "opcionales",
      "title": "Optional liaisons",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "After verbs and some other words, liaison depends on the **register**: more in a speech or when reading aloud, less in conversation."
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Je suis‿allé · Je suis │ allé",
              "tr": "Both are correct."
            },
            {
              "fr": "C'est‿un ami · C'est │ un ami",
              "tr": "Both are correct."
            },
            {
              "fr": "pas‿encore · pas │ encore",
              "tr": "Both are correct."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "To start with, focus on making the required ones and avoiding the forbidden ones. The optional ones will come with listening."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "encadenamiento",
      "title": "Enchaînement",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Don't confuse liaison with **enchaînement** (linking): a consonant that is **already pronounced** joins the vowel of the next word."
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Il‿a",
              "tr": "«ee-la»"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Elle‿aime",
              "tr": "«eh-lem»"
            },
            {
              "fr": "une‿amie",
              "tr": "«ü-na-mee»"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "The difference: in liaison, the consonant was silent and becomes pronounced; in enchaînement it was already pronounced and just moves to the next word."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
